ndent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N(2+(-2),5+2) \\\\  & ∼N(0,7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y\\sim N(0, 7).$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186161859 1071 "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X \\sim N(2,7)$ and $Y \\sim  N(5, 4).$ Find the distribution of the random variable $W = 2X-Y.$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186161859 1071 "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X \\sim N(2,7)$ and $Y \\sim N(5, 4)$. Find the distribution of the random variable $W = 2X-Y$." 13194139535324 17592186045417 false] [17592186161859 1074 "Recall that if $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent random variables, where $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2)$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\naX_1+bX_2\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2, a^2\\sigma^2_1+b^2\\sigma^2_2)\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have\n\n$$\nW = 2X - Y \\mid ∼N(2 \\cdot 2 + (-1) \\cdot 5, 2^{2} \\cdot 7 + (-1)^{2} \\cdot 4); ∼N(-1, 32)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y\\sim N(-1, 32)$." 13194139535324 17592186045417 false] [17592186161859 1074 "Recall that if $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent random variables, where $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\naX_1+bX_2\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2, a^2\\sigma^2_1+b^2\\sigma^2_2).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W=2X-Y & ∼N(2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 5,{2}^{2}\\cdot 7+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 4) \\\\  & ∼N(-1,32).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y\\sim N(-1, 32).$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186161860 1071 "If $X_1\\sim N(10,3^2)$ and $X_2\\sim N(20,5^2)$ are independent and $Y=5X_1-4X_2$, calculate $P(3 \\lt Y \\lt 40)$. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard 