=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.7 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $0.9965$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9966$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9967$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.8 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $0.9974$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9975$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9976$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 2.9 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $0.9981$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9982$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $0.9982$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186161860 1074 "Recall that if $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent random variables, where $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2)$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\naX_1+bX_2\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2, a^2\\sigma^2_1+b^2\\sigma^2_2)\n$$\n\nSince $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nY = 5X_{1} - 4X_{2} \\mid ∼N(5μ_{1} - 4μ_{2}, 5^{2} \\cdot σ_{1}^{2} + 4^{2} \\cdot σ_{2}^{2}); ∼N(5μ_{1} - 4μ_{2}, 25σ_{1}^{2} + 16σ_{2}^{2}); ∼N(5 \\cdot 10 + (-4) \\cdot 20, 25 \\cdot 3^{2} + 16 \\cdot 5^{2}); ∼N(-30, 625)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu = -30$ and standard deviation\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{625} = 25\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3 \\lt Y \\lt 40)$. Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(3 < Y < 40) &= P((3 - (-30))/(25) < Z < (40 - (-30))/(25)) \\\\\n&= P(1.32 < Z < 2.8) \\\\\n&= P(Z < 2.8) - P(Z < 1.32) \\\\\n&= Φ(2.8) - Φ(1.32)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Φ(1.32) & \\approx 0.9066 \\\\ Φ(2.8) & \\approx 0.9974\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(3 < Y < 40) &= Φ(2.8) - Φ(1.32) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.9974 - 0.9066 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.09\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 false] [17592186161860 1074 "Recall that if $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent random variables, where $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\naX_1+bX_2\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2, a^2\\sigma^2_1+b^2\\sigma^2_2).\n$$\n\nSince $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=5{X}_{1}-4{X}_{2} & ∼N(5{\\mu }_{1}-4{\\mu }_{2},{5}^{2}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(5{\\mu }_{1}-4{\\mu }_{2},25{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+16{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(5\\cdot 10+(-4)\\cdot 20,25\\cdot {3}^{2}+16\\cdot {5}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(-30,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu = -30$ and standard deviation $\\sigma = \\sqrt{625} = 25.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(3 \\lt Y \\lt  40).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<Y<40) & =P(\\frac{3-(-30)}{25}<Z<\\frac{40-(-30)}{25}) \\\\  & =P(1.32<Z<2.8) \\\\  & =P(Z<2.8)-P(Z<1.32) \\\\  & =Φ(2.8)-Φ(1.32)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.32) & ≈0.9066 \\\\ Φ(2.8) & ≈0.9974.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<Y<40) & =Φ(2.8)-Φ(1.32) \\\\  & ≈0.9974-0.9066 \\\\  & ≈0.09.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186161861 1071 "The math test results of a group of students are normally distributed with a mean of $67$ and a standard deviation of $12$. The results of the same group on a history test are normally distributed with a mean of $73$ and a standard deviation of $9$. If one student is randomly selected, what is the probability their combined score from both tests is more than $150$? You may assume that the student's scores on both tests are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7123 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7157 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7190 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7224 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7517 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7549 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7823 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7852 \\,$ |" 13194139535324 17592186045417 false] [17592186161861 1071 "The math test results of a group of students are normally distributed with a mean of $67$ and a standard deviation of $12.$ The results of the same group on a history test are normally distributed with a mean of $73$ and a standard deviation of $9.$ If one student is randomly selected, what is the probability their combined score from both tests is more than $150?$ You may assume that the student's scores on both tests are independent. \n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7123 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7157 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7190 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7224 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7517 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7549 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7823 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7852 \\,$ |" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186161861 1074 "Let $X$ denote the student's math test result, and let $Y$ denote the student's history test result. We want to find\n\n$$\nP(X+Y > 150)\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X\\sim N(67,12^2)$ and $Y\\sim N(73,9^2)$, then\n\n$X + Y \\mid ∼N(67 + 73, 12^{2} + 9^{2}); ∼N(140, 225)$.\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu = 140$ and standard deviation\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{225} = 15\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y > 150)$, we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X + Y > 150) &= P(Z > \\frac{150 - 140}{15}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(Z > 0.67) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z \\le 0.67) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(0.67)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.67)\\approx 0.7486\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X + Y > 150) \\mid \\approx 1 - Φ(0.67); \\approx 1 - 0.7486; = 0.2514\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the combined score is more than $150$ is $0.2514$." 13194139535324 17592186045417 false] [17592186161861 1074 "Let $X$ denote the student's math test result, and let $Y$ denote the student's history test result. We want to find \n\n$$\nP( X+Y > 150).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X\\sim N(67,12^2)$ and $Y\\sim N(73,9^2),$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X+Y & ∼N(67+73,{12}^{2}+{9}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(140,225).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu = 140$ and standard deviation $\\sigma = \\sqrt{225} = 15.$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y > 150),$ we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>150) & =P(Z>\\frac{150-140}{15}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.67) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.67) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.67)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.67 )\\approx 0.7486.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X+Y>150) & ≈1-Φ(0.67) \\\\  & ≈1-0.7486 \\\\  & =0.2514.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the combined score is more than $150$ is $0.2514.$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186162002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496246 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186162002 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496311 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186162003 1038 #edb/ref 17592186496243 13194139535324 17