></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6179\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6217\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6255\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6293\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6331\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6554\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6628\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6664\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6700\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2420\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2389\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2358\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2327\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2743\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2709\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2676\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2643\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3085\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3050\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3015\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2981\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186162290 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent random variables, where ${X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2})$ and ${X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),$ and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\na{X}_{1}+b{X}_{2}∼N(a{\\mu }_{1}+b{\\mu }_{2},{a}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{b}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}).\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1}$ and ${X}_{2}$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=2{X}_{1}-3{X}_{2} & ∼N(2{\\mu }_{1}-3{\\mu }_{2},4{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+9{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(4-3,28+36) \\\\  & ∼N(1,64).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =1$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{64}=8.$ \n\nWe want to find $P(-4.6<Y<3.48).$ Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-4.6<Y<3.48) & =P(\\frac{-4.6-1}{8}<Z<\\frac{3.48-1}{8}) \\\\  & =P(-0.70<Z<0.31) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.31)-P(Z<-0.70) \\\\  & =Φ(0.31)-Φ(-0.70)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.31) & ≈0.6217 \\\\ Φ(-0.70) & ≈0.2420.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-4.6<Y<3.48) & ≈Φ(0.31)-Φ(-0.70) \\\\  & ≈0.6217-0.2420 \\\\  & ≈0.3797.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186162297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186162297 1071 "A shop sells spray foam in small and large cans. The volume of liquid in a small can is normally distributed with a mean of $0.3\\,\\text{ltrs}$ and a standard deviation of $0.003\\,\\text{ltrs}.$ The volume of liquid in a large can is normally distributed with a mean of $0.6\\,\\text{ltrs}$ and a standard deviation of $0.008\\,\\text{ltrs}.$\n\nThe foam from a small can expands $40$ times its liquid volume after being sprayed in place, while the foam from a large can expands to $60$ times its liquid volume. \nIf one small and one large can are randomly selected, what is the probability that the total foam volume from both cans after expanding is less than $49\\,\\text{ltrs}?$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9713\\,$ | $\\,0.9719\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ | $\\,0.9732\\,$ | $\\,0.9738\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.9772\\,$ | $\\,0.9778\\,$ | $\\,0.9783\\,$ | $\\,0.9788\\,$ | $\\,0.9793\\,$ |\n| $\\,2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.9821\\,$ | $\\,0.9826\\,$ | $\\,0.9830\\,$ | $\\,0.9834\\,$ | $\\,0.9838\\,$ |" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186162297 1071 "A shop sells spray foam in small and large cans. The volume of liquid in a small can is normally distributed with a mean of $0.3ltrs$ and a standard deviation of $0.003ltrs$. The volume of liquid in a large can is normally distributed with a mean of $0.6ltrs$ and a standard deviation of $0.008ltrs$.\n\nThe foam from a small can expands $40$ times its liquid volume after being sprayed in place, while the foam from a large can expands to $60$ times its liquid volume.\nIf one small and one large can are randomly selected, what is the probability that the total foam volume from both cans after expanding is less than $49ltrs$?\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ | $0.04$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1.9$ | $0.9713$ | $0.9719$ | $0.9726$ | $0.9732$ | $0.9738$ |\n| $2.0$ | $0.9772$ | $0.9778$ | $0.9783$ | $0.9788$ | $0.9793$ |\n| $2.1$ | $0.9821$ | $0.9826$ | $0.9830$ | $0.9834$ | $0.9838$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535324 17592186045417 false] [17592186162297 1074 "Let $X$ denote the volume of liquid in a small can, and let $Y$ denote the volume of liquid in a big can. We want to find \n\n$$\nP(40X+60Y<49).\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are  independent random variables such that $X∼N(0.3,{0.003}^{2})$ and $Y∼N(0.6,{0.008}^{2})$, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40X+60Y & ∼N(40\\cdot 0.3+60\\cdot 0.6,{40}^{2}\\cdot {0.003}^{2}+{60}^{2}\\cdot {0.008}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(48,0.2448).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variable $40X+60Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu =48$ and standard deviation $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.2448}.$\n\nIn order to find $P(40X+60Y<49),$ we convert $40X+60Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(40X+60Y<49) & =P(Z<\\frac{49-48}{\\sqrt{0.2448}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<2.02) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.02) \\\\  & =Φ(2.02)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(2.02)≈0.9783.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(40X+60Y<49) & ≈Φ(2.02) \\\\  & ≈0.9783.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that, after expanding, the total volume of foam is less than $49$ liters is $0.9783.$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186162667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161294 13194139535324 17592186045417 false] [17592186163271 1064 "Let $X_1$ and $X_2$ be *independent* random variables that are normally distributed with\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2), \\qquad X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2)\n$$\n\nWe can show that the random variable\n\n$$\nY = X_1\\pm X_2\n$$\n\nis normally distributed, and\n\n$$\nY = X_1\\pm X_2 \\sim N(\\mu_1\\pm\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2)\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this in a separate lesson.\n\nFor example, if $X_1\\sim N(2,3)$ and $X_2\\sim N(1,4)$ are independent, then\n\n$$\nX_{1} + X_{2} \\mid ∼N(2 + 1, 3 + 4)∼N(3, 7)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX_{1} - X_{2} \\mid ∼N(2 - 1, 3 + 4)∼N(1, 7)\n$$\n\nWhen computing a sum or difference of normal random variables, there are a few things to watch out for:\n\n- Firstly, when we compute the distribution of a *difference*, we subtract the means, but *add* the variances!\n- Secondly, it is vital to check that $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent. Cases where $X_1$ and $X_2$ are *dependent* will be dealt with in future lessons." 13194139535324 17592186045417 false] [17592186163271 1064 "Let $X_1$ and $X_2$ be *independent* random variables that are normally distributed with\n\n$$\nX_1\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2), \\qquad X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2).\n$$\n\nWe can show that the random variable $Y = X_1\\pm X_2$ is normally distributed, and\n\n$$\nY =  X_1\\pm X_2 \\sim N(\\mu_1\\pm\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2).\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this in a separate lesson.\n\nFor example, if $X_1\\sim N(2,3)$ and $X_2\\sim  N(1,4)$ are independent, then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}+{X}_{2} & ∼N(2+1,3+4)∼N(3,7),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}-{X}_{2} & ∼N(2-1,3+4)∼N(1,7).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWhen computing a sum or difference of normal random variables, there are a few things to watch out for:\n\n- Firstly, when we compute the distribution of a *difference*, we subtract the means, but *add* the variances!\n\n- Secondly, it is vital to check that $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent. Cases where $X_1$ and $X_2$ are *dependent* will be dealt with in future lessons." 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186163285 1064 "If $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2)$ are *independent* random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\naX_1+bX_2\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2, a^2\\sigma^2_1+b^2\\sigma^2_2)\n$$\n\nFor example, if $X_1\\sim N(2,3)$ and $X_2\\sim N(1,4)$ are independent, then\n\n$$\n2X_{1} + 3X_{2} \\mid ∼N(2 \\cdot 2 + 3 \\cdot 1, 2^{2} \\cdot 3 + 3^{2} \\cdot 4); ∼N(4 + 3, 4 \\cdot 3 + 9 \\cdot 4); ∼N(7, 48)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n2X_{1} - 3X_{2} \\mid ∼N(2 \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot 1, 2^{2} \\cdot 3 + 3^{2} \\cdot 4); ∼N(4 - 3, 4 \\cdot 3 + 9 \\cdot 4); ∼N(1, 48)\n$$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 false] [17592186163285 1064 "If $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1, \\sigma_1^2)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2, \\sigma_2^2)$ are *independent* random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\naX_1+bX_2\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2, a^2\\sigma^2_1+b^2\\sigma^2_2).\n$$\n\