 =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(1.98\\times {10}^{20})\\cdot (3.84\\times {10}^{8})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (5.97\\times {10}^{24})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.98\\cdot (3.84)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 5.97}\\times \\frac{{10}^{20}\\cdot ({10}^{8})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}} \\\\  & =(0.733\\,2…)\\times {10}^{23} \\\\  & =(7.332…)\\times {10}^{22}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the mass of the Moon is\n$(\\,7.33\\times {10}^{22}\\,)\\,\\text{kg}.$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186217790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186217790 1071 "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(6.2 \\cdot 10^{6})kg$ and $(9.5 \\cdot 10^{5})kg$, has a magnitude of $(1.9 \\cdot 10^{-3})N$.\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11})m^{3}/(kg \\cdot s^{2})$.The distance between the two objects is\n$($ {{field-1}}\n$\\cdot$\n{{field-2}} $)m$." 13194139535324 17592186045417 false] [17592186217790 1071 "The gravitational force between two objects, of masses $(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(9.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg},$ has a magnitude of $(1.9\\times {10}^{-3})\\,\\text{N}.$\nAccording to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe distance between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186217790 1074 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(9.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(6.2\\times {10}^{6})\\cdot (9.5\\times {10}^{5})}{1.9\\times {10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 6.2\\cdot 9.5}{1.9}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{6}\\cdot {10}^{5}}{{10}^{-3}} \\\\  & =206.77\\times {10}^{3} \\\\  & =20.677\\times {10}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{20.677\\times {10}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20.677}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{4}} \\\\  & =(4.547…)\\times {10}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the distance between the two objects is\n$(\\,4.5\\times {10}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139535324 17592186045417 true] [17592186233431 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496217 13194139535324 1759218604